1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,502
Chai Corner has six people now.

2
00:00:02,802 --> 00:00:06,087
But Asha gets opposite
orders from Ravi and Kabir.

3
00:00:06,637 --> 00:00:08,763
Two helpers wash the same cups.

4
00:00:08,763 --> 00:00:10,426
Nobody counts the money.

5
00:00:10,726 --> 00:00:12,981
By evening, the cash box is short.

6
00:00:13,631 --> 00:00:15,334
Good chai is not enough.

7
00:00:15,334 --> 00:00:17,835
Ravi needs rules for running the team.

8
00:00:18,535 --> 00:00:21,449
Meet Henri Fayol, a
French mining engineer.

9
00:00:21,799 --> 00:00:27,324
In 1888, he took over a mining
company close to going bust.

10
00:00:27,674 --> 00:00:31,629
He ran it for thirty years, and made
it one of the biggest in Europe.

11
00:00:31,979 --> 00:00:35,908
In 1916, he wrote his
famous book on management.

12
00:00:36,258 --> 00:00:39,270
Many call him the father
of modern management.

13
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:43,227
Fayol saw that every company
does six kinds of work.

14
00:00:43,578 --> 00:00:47,230
Technical, commercial, financial,
security, and accounting.

15
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:49,106
The sixth is managing.

16
00:00:49,106 --> 00:00:51,036
Fayol said it can be taught.

17
00:00:51,686 --> 00:00:54,114
So, what is administrative management?

18
00:00:54,414 --> 00:00:59,005
It means running the whole organisation,
with clear functions and principles.

19
00:00:59,355 --> 00:01:01,396
Taylor studied one worker's task.

20
00:01:01,396 --> 00:01:03,438
Fayol studied the whole company.

21
00:01:04,088 --> 00:01:07,169
Every manager, Fayol said, does five jobs.

22
00:01:07,169 --> 00:01:09,192
Watch Ravi run one Saturday.

23
00:01:09,541 --> 00:01:10,441
Planning.

24
00:01:10,328 --> 00:01:11,435
Looking ahead.

25
00:01:11,435 --> 00:01:14,517
On Friday, Ravi plans
for two hundred cups.

26
00:01:14,867 --> 00:01:15,767
Organising.

27
00:01:15,689 --> 00:01:17,304
Putting people in place.

28
00:01:17,304 --> 00:01:20,848
Asha at the stove, Kabir
on cash, Meena at the sink.

29
00:01:21,198 --> 00:01:22,098
Commanding.

30
00:01:22,083 --> 00:01:23,554
Giving clear orders.

31
00:01:23,554 --> 00:01:26,732
Ravi tells each person
what to do, and when.

32
00:01:27,082 --> 00:01:28,020
Coordinating.

33
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:32,751
Making the work fit together, so tea,
cash and clean cups move in step.

34
00:01:33,101 --> 00:01:34,001
Controlling.

35
00:01:33,949 --> 00:01:35,616
Checking against the plan.

36
00:01:35,616 --> 00:01:38,633
By night, only one hundred
and eighty are sold.

37
00:01:38,983 --> 00:01:41,392
So next Friday, the plan changes.

38
00:01:41,392 --> 00:01:43,393
The five jobs form a cycle.

39
00:01:44,043 --> 00:01:48,272
Next come fourteen principles,
simple rules a manager can follow.

40
00:01:48,622 --> 00:01:50,478
Group one, who does what.

41
00:01:50,478 --> 00:01:54,707
Division of work, authority with
responsibility, and centralisation.

42
00:01:55,057 --> 00:01:57,002
Group two, who gives orders.

43
00:01:57,002 --> 00:02:00,796
Unity of command, unity of
direction, and the scalar chain.

44
00:02:01,146 --> 00:02:03,142
Group three, rules and order.

45
00:02:03,142 --> 00:02:06,697
Discipline, order, and the
common good before your own.

46
00:02:07,047 --> 00:02:09,042
Group four, caring for people.

47
00:02:09,042 --> 00:02:12,985
Fair pay, equity, a steady job,
initiative, and team spirit.

48
00:02:13,635 --> 00:02:16,825
So what do the key principles
look like at work today?

49
00:02:17,175 --> 00:02:18,404
Division of work.

50
00:02:18,404 --> 00:02:21,526
Each person does one
job, and gets good at it.

51
00:02:21,526 --> 00:02:25,772
In a hospital, surgeons, nurses
and lab staff each do their part.

52
00:02:26,122 --> 00:02:27,437
Unity of command.

53
00:02:27,437 --> 00:02:30,448
Each person takes orders
from just one boss.

54
00:02:30,448 --> 00:02:33,879
A new employee reports to
one manager, not three.

55
00:02:34,229 --> 00:02:35,239
Scalar chain.

56
00:02:35,239 --> 00:02:37,428
Orders travel in one clear line.

57
00:02:37,428 --> 00:02:42,064
A shortcut, the gang plank, lets two
team leads talk directly, if their

58
00:02:42,064 --> 00:02:43,074
bosses agree.

59
00:02:43,424 --> 00:02:44,324
Equity.

60
00:02:44,118 --> 00:02:46,432
Treat everyone fairly and kindly.

61
00:02:46,432 --> 00:02:49,567
The same leave rules
apply to every employee.

62
00:02:49,917 --> 00:02:51,083
Esprit de corps.

63
00:02:51,083 --> 00:02:53,132
That is French for team spirit.

64
00:02:53,132 --> 00:02:55,958
A sales team celebrates
a big month together.

65
00:02:56,608 --> 00:02:58,494
Are Fayol's rules perfect?

66
00:02:58,494 --> 00:02:59,438
Not quite.

67
00:02:59,788 --> 00:03:00,960
The good side.

68
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:05,108
Clear roles, one boss each,
and ideas for any organisation.

69
00:03:05,458 --> 00:03:06,508
The hard side.

70
00:03:06,508 --> 00:03:10,672
They see work from the manager's
chair, and say little about how workers

71
00:03:10,672 --> 00:03:11,572
feel.

72
00:03:11,543 --> 00:03:17,066
In 1946, Herbert Simon said some
principles clash, like old proverbs.

73
00:03:17,416 --> 00:03:20,669
One boss each can clash
with advice from experts.

74
00:03:21,019 --> 00:03:24,798
Fayol himself said his principles
are flexible, not rigid.

75
00:03:25,148 --> 00:03:26,752
So the lesson is simple.

76
00:03:26,752 --> 00:03:28,962
Use the rules, but with judgement.

77
00:03:29,612 --> 00:03:30,584
Quick check.

78
00:03:30,584 --> 00:03:34,542
Asha gets orders from Ravi, and
different orders from Kabir.

79
00:03:34,542 --> 00:03:36,336
Which principle is broken?

80
00:03:38,236 --> 00:03:39,511
Unity of command!

81
00:03:39,511 --> 00:03:42,787
Each person should take
orders from just one boss.

82
00:03:43,387 --> 00:03:44,400
So, remember.

83
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:49,433
Every manager plans, organises,
commands, coordinates and controls.

84
00:03:49,783 --> 00:03:53,806
Fourteen principles give clear
roles, one boss, and fair treatment.

85
00:03:54,156 --> 00:03:56,094
But use the rules with judgement.

86
00:03:56,694 --> 00:04:00,803
Next time, we'll meet Max Weber,
and his idea of bureaucracy.
